<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Orange River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gariep River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Orange River is the longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, flowing about 2,200 kilometres from the Lesotho highlands west to the Atlantic, where it forms the border between South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>. In the 18th and 19th centuries the lands north of it were home to Nama, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griqua">Griqua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korana">Korana</a> communities and became a frontier of mission stations, trekkers and colonial expansion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/orange-river.jpg" alt="Orange River" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: paffy · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OrangeRiverUpington.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
